Regular expressions by non-determinism
======================================

A regular expression denotes a set of words.  This example writes that
meaning as a Curry function: sem returns one word of the set, and every word
of the set is one value.  Two more functions, match and grep, use the words
to test a subject and to search it.  A small Python parser turns the usual
pattern syntax into Curry data.


1. What the example shows about functional logic programming
-------------------------------------------------------------

Non-determinism as meaning.  The function sem maps a pattern to one of its
words:

    sem (Lit c)   = [c]
    sem Any       = [c] where c free
    sem (Alt a b) = sem a ? sem b
    sem (Seq a b) = sem a ++ sem b
    sem (Star a)  = [] ? (nonEmpty (sem a) ++ sem (Star a))
    sem (Plus a)  = sem a ++ sem (Star a)
    sem (Opt a)   = [] ? sem a

The choice operator ? stands for alternation and for the two cases of a
star.  The list concatenation ++ stands for sequence.  A free Char stands
for the wildcard.  It unifies with any character of the subject.

Matching by unification.  The function match asks a word of the pattern to
be the subject:

    match r s | sem r =:= s = True

The non-deterministic evaluation of sem r picks the word.  Each unfolding
of a star is a choice.  Unification with the subject prunes a branch at the
first wrong character, so the search is finite.  When no word is the
subject, the guard fails and the call has no value.

Search with free variables.  The function grep splits the subject into three
free parts and asks the middle part to be a word:

    grep r s | pre ++ hit ++ post =:= s & hit =:= sem r = (pre, hit, post)
      where pre, hit, post free

Narrowing binds the free variables: the call pre ++ hit ++ post =:= s
instantiates them to each split of the subject into three parts.  The
bindings of pre, hit and post say where the hit is.  Each hit is one value
of the call.  The module has no matching automaton and no code for
backtracking.

A star repeats only non-empty words of its body.  The helper nonEmpty fails
on the empty word.  Without it a body that matches the empty word, as in
(a?)*, would unfold forever and consume no character.  The language stays
the same.


2. What the example shows about calling Curry from Python
----------------------------------------------------------

The driver go.py adds its own directory to curry.path and imports the module
Regex.  It parses a pattern with a recursive-descent parser of about forty
lines.  The syntax: a character stands for itself, . is any character, | is
alternation, * + ? repeat the item before them, parentheses group, and a
backslash makes the next character literal.  The parser returns nested
tuples.  The first item of each tuple is the name of a constructor of
Regex.RE.  The function build turns each tuple into Curry data:

    curry.expr(getattr(Regex, tag), *args)

The argument of Lit is a Python str of length one, which curry.expr turns
into a Char.  For the same reason go.py passes every subject as a list of
characters, text(subject).

The Python function match takes the first value of the goal and stops the
search.  A goal with no value means False.  A failed guard is not an error.
The Python function grep iterates over every value of the goal with
converter='topython', which turns the tuple of three Strings into Python
strings.  Curry does not promise an order of the values, so go.py sorts the
hits by position and length.  A pattern such as a*a* derives one hit in
several ways.  Each derivation is one value, so go.py keeps one copy of each
hit.


3. How to run
-------------

Run "make stage" from the root of the repository first.  Then run

    ./run

The run script uses the C++ backend by default.  To pick a backend, set
SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS in the environment:

    SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS=backend:py ./run
    SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS=backend:cxx ./run

To try your own pattern and subject, pass both to go.py.  It runs match and
grep on the pair.  Quote the pattern for the shell:

    ../../install/bin/python go.py 'a.*c' xabcacx


4. Expected output
------------------

The file expected.out holds the exact output of ./run.  The key lines are:

    match ab*c abbbc: True
    match a.c axc: True
    match a.c abbc: False
    grep ab*c in xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxabbbcyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy: 1 hit
      xxxxxxxxxxxxxxx[abbbc]yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy
    grep a+b? in zzaaabzz: 9 hits
      zz[a]aabzz
      zz[aa]abzz
      ...
      zzaa[ab]zz
    grep (cat|dog)s? in cats and dogs and a dog: 5 hits
      [cat]s and dogs and a dog
      [cats] and dogs and a dog
      ...

grep lists every substring that is a word of the pattern, not only the
longest one at each position.  So a+b? has nine hits in zzaaabzz, and
(cat|dog)s? finds both cat and cats.  The output is identical on both
backends.


5. Limits
---------

A warm run of ./run took 4 s on the Python backend and 0.3 s on the C++
backend.  The first run also compiles the module.  It took 12 s.

The split pre ++ hit ++ post has a quadratic number of cases, so grep slows
with the length of the subject.  On the Python backend grep of ab*c took 3 s
in a 41-character subject and 20 s in an 80-character subject.  On the C++
backend the 80-character subject took 1 s.  A pattern with many hits costs
more.  The pattern .*b.* has 453 hits in a 40-character subject.  It took
22 s on the Python backend and 1 s on the C++ backend.  Keep subjects under
about 40 characters on the Python backend.

On the C++ backend the memory of grep grows fast with the length of the
subject.  Measured with go.py 'ab*c' on a subject of x's: a peak of 0.9 GB
at 100 characters and 1.7 GB at 120.  A subject of 130 characters fails in
grep with MemoryError: std::bad_alloc under a 2 GiB address-space cap,
before the recursion limit below is reached.

curry.expr builds a list by recursion, one element per level, so a subject
longer than about 140 characters exceeds the Python recursion limit on either
backend.
